 ## Overview
 
-The Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) traversal is a grid traversal algorithm that determines the sequence of axis-aligned grid cells (voxels or tiles) intersected by a ray [S1, unofficial][S4, unofficial][S7, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently locating geometry in a dense grid without performing expensive per-voxel intersection tests or suffering from the gaps and redundancies of naive fixed-step ray marching [S31, S41, unofficial]. Unlike [Siddon's Algorithm](2590-siddon-s-algorithm.md), which computes exact parametric intersection lengths for integration, DDA steps discretely through grid cells using incremental comparisons, making it ideal for real-time ray casting and shadow calculation [S1, S25, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [3D Bresenham Voxel Traversal](3951-3d-bresenham-voxel-tracing.md) by using floating-point $t$-values for boundary distances rather than integer error accumulation, allowing it to handle arbitrary ray directions and non-unit grid spacing more naturally [S3, S31, unofficial].
