 ### [Amanatides-Woo Voxel Traversal](3132-amanatides-woo-voxel-traversal.md)
-The Amanatides-Woo algorithm is a fast, branch-minimal method for traversing a uniform 3D voxel grid along a ray, visiting every cell intersected by the ray in front-to-back order. It solves the problem of efficiently locating geometry in a dense grid without performing expensive per-voxel intersection tests or suffering from the gaps and redundancies of naive fixed-step ray marching. Unlike [Siddon's Algorithm](2590-siddon-s-algorithm.md), which computes exact parametric intersection lengths for integration, Amanatides-Woo steps discretely through grid cells using incremental comparisons, making it ideal for real-time ray casting and shadow calculation. It sets itself apart from [3D Bresenham Voxel Traversal](3949-3d-bresenham-voxel-tracing.md) by using floating-point $t$-values for boundary distances rather than integer error accumulation, allowing it to handle arbitrary ray directions and non-unit grid spacing more naturally.
+Amanatides-Woo is a fast, branch-minimal algorithm for traversing a uniform 3D voxel grid along a ray, visiting only the voxels intersected by the ray in $O(n_x + n_y + n_z)$ time. It solves the problem of efficiently locating geometry in a dense grid without performing expensive per-voxel intersection tests or suffering from the precision errors and missed voxels inherent in naive fixed-step ray marching. It sets itself apart from its siblings by using floating-point parametric distances ($t$-values) to determine the next voxel boundary crossing based on the minimum time to cross any axis boundary, guaranteeing that the ray stops and tests a voxel index at exactly one of the x, y, or z dividing planes.
 
 ### [3D Bresenham Voxel Traversal](3951-3d-bresenham-voxel-traversal.md)
-3D Bresenham Voxel Traversal is a discrete line algorithm used to determine the sequence of integer voxel coordinates intersected by a line segment in a 3D grid. It iterates along the dominant axis and updates the other two axes based on accumulated error terms, effectively rasterizing the ray path through the voxel space. Unlike continuous DDA methods that guarantee every voxel the ray physically passes through is visited, Bresenham-based traversal may skip blocks where the ray does not get close enough to the block center, making it faster but potentially incomplete for volume rendering. It is often implemented as the stepping logic within Half-Vector DDA (HDDA) structures.
+3D Bresenham Voxel Traversal is a discrete line algorithm used to determine the sequence of integer voxel coordinates intersected by a line segment in a 3D grid. It rasterizes a line between two integer coordinates by iterating along the dominant axis and updating the other two axes based on accumulated error terms. It differs from its parent [DDA](2323-dda.md) and siblings like [Amanatides-Woo Voxel Traversal](3132-amanatides-woo-voxel-traversal.md) by operating entirely in integer arithmetic (or fixed-point logic derived from it) rather than floating-point plane intersections, making it computationally lighter but topologically distinct: it does not consider every block the ray passes through, skipping those where the ray does not get close enough to the block center.
 
